\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.1}{\ignorespaces The DreamerV3 world model and actor-critic (Hafner et al., 2023). Observations are encoded into a compact latent state whose evolution a sequence model predicts from actions; the actor and critic then learn on trajectories \emph  {imagined} inside the model rather than in the real environment. Predictions are conditioned on the agent's \emph  {native} action, the body-specific conditioning signal this thesis replaces with a latent action.}}{7}{figure.caption.6}\protected@file@percent }
\providecommand*\caption@xref[2]{\@setref\relax\@undefined{#1}}
\newlabel{fig:dreamer}{{2.1}{7}{The DreamerV3 world model and actor-critic (Hafner et al., 2023). Observations are encoded into a compact latent state whose evolution a sequence model predicts from actions; the actor and critic then learn on trajectories \emph {imagined} inside the model rather than in the real environment. Predictions are conditioned on the agent's \emph {native} action, the body-specific conditioning signal this thesis replaces with a latent action}{figure.caption.6}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {2.2.2}Video foundation models and the JEPA objective}{8}{subsection.2.2.2}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\numberline {2.3}Latent Action Models and Latent Action World Models}{8}{section.2.3}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {2.3.1}Inverse and forward dynamics models: the general building block}{8}{subsection.2.3.1}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.2}{\ignorespaces V-JEPA~2 multistage training (Assran et al., 2025). Left: self-supervised pretraining on internet-scale video with a mask-prediction objective in \emph  {representation} space (the JEPA objective), which favours motion-relevant features. Right: the encoder is then frozen and an action-conditioned predictor (V-JEPA~2-AC) is learned on top. This project reuses only the frozen encoder as a per-frame visual front-end (Section 3.3).}}{9}{figure.caption.7}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:vjepa2}{{2.2}{9}{V-JEPA~2 multistage training (Assran et al., 2025). Left: self-supervised pretraining on internet-scale video with a mask-prediction objective in \emph {representation} space (the JEPA objective), which favours motion-relevant features. Right: the encoder is then frozen and an action-conditioned predictor (V-JEPA~2-AC) is learned on top. This project reuses only the frozen encoder as a per-frame visual front-end (Section 3.3)}{figure.caption.7}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {2.3.2}The idea of a latent action}{9}{subsection.2.3.2}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.3}{\ignorespaces Overview of UniSkill (Kim et al., 2025). (a) An Inverse Skill Dynamics model and a Forward Skill Dynamics model are jointly trained on diverse video to encode a body-independent skill representation; (b) a skill-conditioned policy is trained on robot data; (c) skills extracted from a human-video prompt are executed on the robot to reproduce the behaviour. The inverse/forward pairing matches this project's ITM/FTM (Section 3.4); the difference is that UniSkill \emph  {aligns} its representation across bodies by training on cross-embodiment data, whereas this project tests whether such invariance \emph  {emerges} without any alignment objective.}}{11}{figure.caption.8}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:uniskill}{{2.3}{11}{Overview of UniSkill (Kim et al., 2025). (a) An Inverse Skill Dynamics model and a Forward Skill Dynamics model are jointly trained on diverse video to encode a body-independent skill representation; (b) a skill-conditioned policy is trained on robot data; (c) skills extracted from a human-video prompt are executed on the robot to reproduce the behaviour. The inverse/forward pairing matches this project's ITM/FTM (Section 3.4); the difference is that UniSkill \emph {aligns} its representation across bodies by training on cross-embodiment data, whereas this project tests whether such invariance \emph {emerges} without any alignment objective}{figure.caption.8}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {2.3.3}Latent Action World Models (the base method)}{11}{subsection.2.3.3}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.4}{\ignorespaces LAC-WM architecture (Huang et al., 2026), the pipeline this thesis adapts. During pretraining an inverse model encodes an observation pair into a unified latent action that conditions a forward model, and a motion decoder maps the latent back to each embodiment's motor labels; a finetuning-stage action projector lets the world model also condition on an unseen body's raw actions. The paper's inverse/forward \emph  {dynamics} models are what this project instantiates on visual embeddings and renames the Inverse/Forward \emph  {Transition} Model (Section 3.4).}}{12}{figure.caption.9}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:lacwm}{{2.4}{12}{LAC-WM architecture (Huang et al., 2026), the pipeline this thesis adapts. During pretraining an inverse model encodes an observation pair into a unified latent action that conditions a forward model, and a motion decoder maps the latent back to each embodiment's motor labels; a finetuning-stage action projector lets the world model also condition on an unseen body's raw actions. The paper's inverse/forward \emph {dynamics} models are what this project instantiates on visual embeddings and renames the Inverse/Forward \emph {Transition} Model (Section 3.4)}{figure.caption.9}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\numberline {2.4}Cross-Morphology and Cross-Embodiment Legged Locomotion}{12}{section.2.4}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {2.4.1}Per-body retraining}{12}{subsection.2.4.1}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {2.4.2}Explicit morphology conditioning}{13}{subsection.2.4.2}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.5}{\ignorespaces The QWM framework (Danesh et al., 2026). A Physical Morphology Encoder derives a static embedding from each robot's USD design file and conditions the world model on it (left); a policy is then learned entirely in imagination for a new morphology (middle) and deployed by injecting that morphology's embedding (right). The morphology is \emph  {supplied} from a design file, exactly the privileged input this thesis removes by inferring behaviour from external vision.}}{13}{figure.caption.10}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:qwm}{{2.5}{13}{The QWM framework (Danesh et al., 2026). A Physical Morphology Encoder derives a static embedding from each robot's USD design file and conditions the world model on it (left); a policy is then learned entirely in imagination for a new morphology (middle) and deployed by injecting that morphology's embedding (right). The morphology is \emph {supplied} from a design file, exactly the privileged input this thesis removes by inferring behaviour from external vision}{figure.caption.10}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {2.4.3}Online system identification}{13}{subsection.2.4.3}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {2.4.4}Shared policy with per-robot adapters}{13}{subsection.2.4.4}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.6}{\ignorespaces The L3P framework (Zheng et al., 2025). A shared latent policy backbone is wrapped by a per-robot observation encoder (raw sensing $\to $ shared latent space) and a per-robot latent-action decoder (latent action $\to $ robot-specific motor command), with recovery modules enforcing latent-space consistency. This shared-latent-with-body-specific-heads structure parallels this project's latent action plus Motion Decoder, but L3P operates on proprioception and must fit an encoder and decoder per robot.}}{14}{figure.caption.11}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:l3p}{{2.6}{14}{The L3P framework (Zheng et al., 2025). A shared latent policy backbone is wrapped by a per-robot observation encoder (raw sensing $\to $ shared latent space) and a per-robot latent-action decoder (latent action $\to $ robot-specific motor command), with recovery modules enforcing latent-space consistency. This shared-latent-with-body-specific-heads structure parallels this project's latent action plus Motion Decoder, but L3P operates on proprioception and must fit an encoder and decoder per robot}{figure.caption.11}{}}
